@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.862 = private unnamed_addr constant [995 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for conditions that position a constant on the left-hand side of the\0Acomparison operator, rather than the right-hand side.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThese conditions (sometimes referred to as \22Yoda conditions\22) are less\0Areadable than conditions that place the variable on the left-hand side of\0Athe comparison operator.\0A\0AIn some languages, Yoda conditions are used to prevent accidental\0Aassignment in conditions (i.e., accidental uses of the `=` operator,\0Ainstead of the `==` operator). However, Python does not allow assignments\0Ain conditions unless using the `:=` operator, so Yoda conditions provide\0Ano benefit in this regard.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aif \22Foo\22 == foo:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aif foo == \22Foo\22:\0A    ...\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Comparisons](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#comparisons)\0A- [Python documentation: Assignment statements](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#assignment-statements)\0A", align 1
