@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.889 = private unnamed_addr constant [2301 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of except handling via `try`-`except` within `for` and\0A`while` loops.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AException handling via `try`-`except` blocks incurs some performance\0Aoverhead, regardless of whether an exception is raised.\0A\0ATo optimize your code, two techniques are possible:\0A1. Refactor your code to put the entire loop into the `try`-`except` block,\0A   rather than wrapping each iteration in a separate `try`-`except` block.\0A2. Use \22Look Before You Leap\22 idioms that attempt to avoid exceptions\0A   being raised in the first place, avoiding the need to use `try`-`except`\0A   blocks in the first place.\0A\0AThis rule is only enforced for Python versions prior to 3.11, which\0Aintroduced \22zero-cost\22 exception handling. However, note that even on\0APython 3.11 and newer, refactoring your code to avoid exception handling in\0Atight loops can provide a significant speedup in some cases, as zero-cost\0Aexception handling is only zero-cost in the \22happy path\22 where no exception\0Ais raised in the `try`-`except` block.\0A\0AAs with all `perflint` rules, this is only intended as a\0Amicro-optimization. In many cases, it will have a negligible impact on\0Aperformance.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Astring_numbers: list[str] = [\221\22, \222\22, \22three\22, \224\22, \225\22]\0A\0A# `try`/`except` that could be moved out of the loop:\0Aint_numbers: list[int] = []\0Afor num in string_numbers:\0A    try:\0A        int_numbers.append(int(num))\0A    except ValueError as e:\0A        print(f\22Couldn't convert to integer: {e}\22)\0A        break\0A\0A# `try`/`except` used when \22look before you leap\22 idioms could be used:\0Anumber_names: dict[int, str] = {1: \22one\22, 3: \22three\22, 4: \22four\22}\0Afor number in range(5):\0A    try:\0A        name = number_names[number]\0A    except KeyError:\0A        continue\0A    else:\0A        print(f\22The name of {number} is {name}\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Astring_numbers: list[str] = [\221\22, \222\22, \22three\22, \224\22, \225\22]\0A\0Aint_numbers: list[int] = []\0Atry:\0A    for num in string_numbers:\0A        int_numbers.append(int(num))\0Aexcept ValueError as e:\0A    print(f\22Couldn't convert to integer: {e}\22)\0A\0Anumber_names: dict[int, str] = {1: \22one\22, 3: \22three\22, 4: \22four\22}\0Afor number in range(5):\0A    name = number_names.get(number)\0A    if name is not None:\0A        print(f\22The name of {number} is {name}\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A", align 1
